"Web Animations Module Level 1 (https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/)\nciteturn1263view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/web-animations-1/\",\"lineno\":1758}); Total lines: 4503\nL1729: repeatable list\nL1731: Note: The repeatable list concept ensures that a list that is conceptually repeated to a certain length (as cite366†background-origin is repeated to the length of the cite367†background-image list) or repeated infinitely will smoothly transition between any values, and so that the computed value will properly represent the result (and potentially be inherited correctly).\nL1732: (See prose)\nL1733:     Some properties have specific interpolation behavior not covered by the above cases; in this case the animation behavior will be specified explicitly for that property.\nL1734: \nL1735: The cite358†animation type of properties that do not yet include an Animation type line in their property definition, is defined in cite145†Appendix A: Animation types of existing properties .\nL1736: #### 5.2.1. Custom Properties\nL1737: For cite368†custom properties registered using the `cite369†registerProperty()†drafts.css-houdini.org ` method for the cite296†current global object†html.spec.whatwg.org , the cite358†animation type is cite365†by computed value , derived from the type used in the property’s cite370†syntax definition†drafts.css-houdini.org . Where there is no cite362†computed value type that corresponds to the property’s specified syntax (e.g.\nL1738: when the syntax is the cite371†universal syntax definition†drafts.css-houdini.org ) or when the custom property is not registered, the animation type is cite364†discrete .\nL1739: ### 5.3. Keyframe effects\nL1740: \nL1741: Keyframe effects are a kind of cite174†animation effect that uses the output of the timing model to update CSS properties of an element or cite317†pseudo-element (such as `::before` or `::after` cite372†[select] ) referred to as the effect target.\nL1742: The cite312†effect target is comprised of an `cite373†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org ` known as the target element and a cite317†pseudo-element selector known as the target pseudo-selector. If the effect target is an `cite373†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org `, the cite374†target element is that element and the cite375†target pseudo-selector is null. If the effect target is a pseudo-element, the target element is its cite376†originating element and the target pseudo-selector is one that specifies that particular pseudo-element.\nL1743: Note that not all cite312†effect targets specified in this manner (such as cite377†::part() pseudo-elements and unsupported pseudo-elements) have computed property values defined.\nL1744: #### 5.3.1. Keyframes\nL1745: \nL1746: The cite378†effect values for a cite237†keyframe effect are calculated by interpolating between a series of property values positioned at fractional offsets. Each set of property values indexed by an offset is called a keyframe.\nL1747: The offset of a keyframe is a value in the range [0, 1] or the special value null. The list of cite379†keyframes for a cite237†keyframe effect must be loosely sorted by offset, which means that for each keyframe in the list that has a cite380†keyframe offset that is not null, the offset must be greater than or equal to the offset of the previous keyframe in the list with a keyframe offset that is not null, if any.\nL1748: The behavior when cite379†keyframes overlap or have unsupported values is defined in cite103†§ 5.3.4 The effect value of a keyframe effect .\nL1749: \nL1750: Each keyframe also has a keyframe-specific easing function associated with it, which is an cite336†easing function that is applied to the period of time between the keyframe on which it is specified and the next keyframe in the list.\nL1751: \nL1752: Note: The cite336†easing function specified on the last keyframe in the list is never applied.\nL1753: Each cite379†keyframe may have a keyframe-specific composite operation that, if set, is applied to all values specified in that keyframe. The possible operations and their meanings are identical to those defined for the cite381†composite operation associated with the cite237†keyframe effect as a whole in cite108†§ 5.4.4 Effect composition .\nL1754: If the cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation for a keyframe is not set, the composite operation specified for the keyframe effect as a whole is used for values specified in that keyframe.\nL1755: Tests\nL1756: \nL1757:   * cite383†applying-interpolated-transform.html†wpt.fyi cite384†(live test)†wpt.live cite385†(source)†github.com L1758: #### 5.3.2. Computing property values\nL1759: \nL1760: To compute a property value given a property `property`, a value `value`, and an `cite373†Element†dom.spec.whatwg.org ` `element`: resolve `value` according to the “Computed Value” line of the `property`’s definition table, using the cite362†computed values of `element` as the context for resolving dependencies, and return the result.\nL1761: \nL1762: Note: The cite362†computed values on `element` are not affected by this algorithm.\nL1763: This algorithm implies that property values specified in keyframes can establish order dependencies. When cite386†computing a property value , the cite362†computed values of dependencies held by `value` must be calculated first.\nL1764: \nL1765:     var animation = elem.animate([{ fontSize: '10px', width: '10em' },\nL1766:                                   { fontSize: '20px', width: '20em' }], 1000);\nL1767:     animation.currentTime = 500;\nL1768:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).fontSize); // Should be 15px\nL1769:     console.log(getComputedStyle(elem).width); // Should be 225px\nL1770: In this example, in order to cite386†compute a property value for `10em`, we need to know the cite362†computed value of cite387†font-size on the cite374†target element , which in turn is determined by the cite378†effect value for font-size, which in turn depends on computing property values for font-size. Hence, computing property values are subject to ordering constraints.\nL1771: #### 5.3.3. Calculating computed keyframes\nL1772: \nL1773: Before calculating the cite378†effect value of a cite237†keyframe effect , the property values on its cite379†keyframes are cite101†computed , and the offset to use for any keyframes with a null cite380†keyframe offset is computed. The result of resolving these values is a set of computed keyframes.\nL1774: \nL1775: The calculated cite380†keyframe offsets of a set of cite379†keyframe that includes suitable values for each null keyframe offset are referred to as the computed keyframe offsets.\nL1776: To produce cite388†computed keyframe offsets , we define a procedure to compute missing keyframe offsets that takes a sequence of cite379†keyframes , `keyframes`, and has the following steps:\nL1777: \nL1778:   1. For each cite379†keyframe in `keyframes`, let the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the keyframe be equal to its cite380†keyframe offset value.\nL1779:   2. If `keyframes` contains more than one cite379†keyframe and the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set the computed keyframe offset of the first keyframe to 0.\nL1780: \nL1781:   3. If the cite388†computed keyframe offset of the last cite379†keyframe in `keyframes` is null, set its computed keyframe offset to 1.\nL1782: \nL1783:   4. For each pair of cite379†keyframes `A` and `B` where:\nL1784: \nL1785:      * `A` appears before `B` in `keyframes`, and\nL1786:      * `A` and `B` have a cite388†computed keyframe offset that is not null, and\nL1787: \nL1788:      * all cite379†keyframes between `A` and `B` have a null cite388†computed keyframe offset ,\nL1789: \nL1790: calculate the cite388†computed keyframe offset of each cite379†keyframe between `A` and `B` as follows:\nL1791: \nL1792:     1. Let offset_{`k`} be the cite388†computed keyframe offset of a cite379†keyframe `k`.\nL1793: \nL1794:     2. Let `n` be the number of keyframes between and including `A` and `B` minus 1.\nL1795:     3. Let `index` refer to the position of `keyframe` in the sequence of keyframes between `A` and `B` such that the first keyframe after `A` has an `index` of 1.\nL1796: \nL1797:     4. Set the cite388†computed keyframe offset of `keyframe` to cite389†offset _{`A`} + (offset_{`B`} − offset_{`A`}) × `index` / `n`.\nL1798: \nL1799: Tests\nL1800:   * cite390†background-shorthand.html†wpt.fyi cite391†(live test)†wpt.live cite392†(source)†github.com L1801:   * cite393†computed-keyframes-shorthands.html†wpt.fyi cite394†(live test)†wpt.live cite395†(source)†github.com L1802:   * cite396†effect-value-context-filling.html†wpt.fyi cite397†(live test)†wpt.live cite398†(source)†github.com L1803:   * cite399†effect-value-context.html†wpt.fyi cite400†(live test)†wpt.live cite401†(source)†github.com L1804: cite402†Computed keyframes are produced using the following procedure. Note that this procedure is only performed on a cite237†keyframe effect having an cite312†effect target for which computed property values can be calculated.\nL1805: \nL1806:   1. Let `computed keyframes` be an empty list of cite379†keyframes .\nL1807: \nL1808:   2. For each `keyframe` in the list of cite379†keyframes specified on this cite237†keyframe effect , perform the following steps:\nL1809: \nL1810:     1. Add a new empty cite379†keyframe , `computed keyframe`, to `computed keyframes`.\nL1811:     2. For each property specified in `keyframe`:\nL1812: \nL1813:        * cite386†Compute a property value using the value specified on `keyframe` as the value, and the cite374†target element as the element; then add the property and resulting value to `computed keyframe`.\nL1814: \nL1815:        * For shorthand properties, add the equivalent longhand properties.\nL1816:        * For logical properties cite403†[CSS-LOGICAL-1] , add the cite404†equivalent physical properties cite405†[CSS-WRITING-MODES-4] based on the computed value of cite406†writing-mode and/or cite407†direction for the cite312†effect target .\nL1817: For example, if `keyframe` has a value of 12pt for the cite408†border-width cite409†shorthand property , the user agent will cite386†compute a property value of 16px for each of the cite410†longhand properties : cite411†border-bottom-width , cite412†border-left-width , cite413†border-right-width , and cite414†border-top-width . As a result, `computed keyframe` would not have a value for the border-width property, but would instead include each of the longhand properties, each with the value 16px.\nL1818: If conflicts arise when expanding shorthand properties or replacing logical properties with physical properties, apply the following rules in order until the conflict is resolved:\nL1819: \nL1820:       1. Longhand properties override shorthand properties (e.g. cite415†border-top-color overrides cite416†border-top ).\nL1821: \nL1822:       2. Shorthand properties with fewer longhand components override those with more longhand components (e.g. cite416†border-top overrides cite417†border-color ).\nL1823:       3. Physical properties override logical properties.\nL1824: \nL1825:       4. For shorthand properties with an equal number of longhand components, properties whose IDL name (see the cite418†CSS property to IDL attribute algorithm cite419†[CSSOM] ) appears earlier when sorted in ascending order by the Unicode codepoints that make up each IDL name, override those who appear later.\nL1826: \nL1827:   3. Apply the procedure to cite420†compute missing keyframe offsets to `computed keyframes`.\nL1828: \nL1829:   4. Return `computed keyframes`.\nL1830: #### 5.3.4. The effect value of a keyframe effect\nL1831: \nL1832: The cite378†effect value of a single property referenced by a cite237†keyframe effect as one of its cite186†target properties , for a given `iteration progress`, `iteration index` and `underlying value` is calculated as follows.\nL1833: \nL1834:   1. If `iteration progress` is cite180†unresolved abort this procedure.\nL1835: \nL1836:   2. Let `target property` be the cite410†longhand property for which the cite378†effect value is to be calculated.\nL1837:   3. If cite358†animation type of the `target property` is cite359†not animatable abort this procedure since the effect cannot be applied.\nL1838: \nL1839:   4. If the cite237†keyframe effect does not have an cite312†effect target , or if the effect target cannot have computed property values calculated, abort this procedure.\nL1840: \nL1841:   5. Let `property-specific keyframes` be the result of getting the set of cite402†computed keyframes for this cite237†keyframe effect .\nL1842:   6. Remove any cite379†keyframes from `property-specific keyframes` that do not have a property value for `target property`.\nL1843: \nL1844:   7. If `property-specific keyframes` is empty, return `underlying value`.\nL1845:   8. If there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 0, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 0, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and prepend it to the beginning of `property-specific keyframes`.\nL1846:   9. Similarly, if there is no cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 1, create a new keyframe with a computed keyframe offset of 1, a property value set to `underlying value`, a cite382†keyframe-specific composite operation of cite421†replace , and a cite340†keyframe-specific easing function of the cite339†linear easing function , and append it to the end of `property-specific keyframes`.\nL1847: Other specifications may override the implicit keyframe generation behavior in the preceding two steps. For example, the cite422†named timeline range keyframe selectors defined by Scroll Animations can cause these implicit keyframes not to be generated.\nL1848: \nL1849: A future level of this specification should incorporate the overridden behavior directly into this algorithm.\nL1850: \nL1851:   1. Let `interval endpoints` be an empty sequence of keyframes.\nL1852:   2. Populate `interval endpoints` by following the steps from the first matching condition below:\nL1853: \nL1854: If `iteration progress` < 0 and there is more than one cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 0,\nL1855: \nL1856: \nL1857: Add the first cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` to `interval endpoints`.\nL1858: \nL1859: If `iteration progress` ≥ 1 and there is more than one cite379†keyframe in `property-specific keyframes` with a cite388†computed keyframe offset of 1,\n"
